From e9cd7ff6ebb9a0e1a6963b4c2cb52e6f154655c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anandu Krishnan E <anandu.e@oss.qualcomm.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 15:09:47 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] misc: fastrpc: fix context leak and hang on
 signal-interrupted invoke

fastrpc invokes work by sending an RPC message to the DSP and blocking
in wait_for_completion_interruptible() until the DSP responds. If a
signal arrives during this wait, the syscall returns -ERESTARTSYS and
the invoke context, which holds the in-flight DMA buffers and
completion state, is left stranded in fl->pending.

On the next syscall attempt (either auto-restarted by the kernel via
SA_RESTART or manually retried by user-space after EINTR), a fresh
context is allocated and the RPC message is re-sent to the DSP. This
has two consequences:

 - The original context leaks in fl->pending until the file is closed.
 - The DSP receives a duplicate invocation. If the DSP was mid-way
   through processing the first request and had issued a reverse RPC
   call back to the host, the retry sends a new forward request
   instead of the expected reverse-RPC response. The DSP thread
   waiting for that response is never woken, causing a hang.

Fix this by saving the interrupted context to a new fl->interrupted
list on -ERESTARTSYS, instead of freeing it or leaving it on
fl->pending. The context keeps the same reference it already held;
only list membership changes, so the refcount stays symmetric no
matter how many times a context is interrupted and retried. When the
same thread retries with a matching handle and sc, the context is
pulled back onto fl->pending and the call jumps straight to the wait,
skipping context allocation and message re-send. A mismatched
handle/sc falls through to a normal fresh allocation, so a thread
that issues a different call after an interrupted one is never
wedged.

A context on fl->interrupted can be matched and reclaimed by
fastrpc_context_restore_interrupted() on a retry while a concurrent
kref_put() from an unrelated drain (SSR, fd close, or a delayed DSP
response) has already dropped its refcount to zero and is waiting on
fl->lock to unlink it in fastrpc_context_free(). Without a check, the
retry can win that lock race, hand back a context already committed
to being freed, and use it after the other side frees it.
kref_get_unless_zero() closes the window: it fails if the refcount is
already zero, so the retry falls through to a fresh allocation
instead of resurrecting a dying context.

Distinguish an interruption of the send itself (rpmsg_send() returning
-ERESTARTSYS before the message ever reaches the DSP) from an
interruption of the wait that follows a successful send: only the
latter is safe to park and resume without resending, since nothing is
actually in flight in the former case. Rather than track this with an
extra flag, give the two failure sites their own cleanup instead of
funnelling both into the same bail: label.

ctx->args is now owned by the context (copied at alloc time) and
freed exactly once by fastrpc_context_free(), rather than aliasing the
caller's array, which could otherwise be freed or reused by the time
a parked context is eventually drained.

A context parked on fl->interrupted is reachable from a resumed retry,
process exit and SSR all at once. fastrpc_context_free() now removes
it from whichever list it is on before releasing its resources, using
list_del_init() (plain list_del() poisons the node, which would
otherwise break this same check if the context is freed via a race
with one of the other drains). Because the DSP response worker
(fastrpc_rpmsg_callback()) and the exit/SSR/fd-close paths can all end
up scheduling the same ctx->put_work for a parked context, a
put_work_scheduled flag checked and set under fl->lock ensures only
one of them actually queues it.

fastrpc_device_release() now also drains fl->interrupted, completing
any parked context with -EPIPE and dropping the invoker's reference,
so a context stuck there at fd close cannot leak it. It cannot drop
the worker reference unconditionally, though: the DSP may still be
using that context's buffers if it hasn't answered yet, and freeing
them under it would be unsafe. fastrpc_release_current_dsp_process(),
already called at the top of fastrpc_device_release(), tells the DSP
to tear down the whole process; if that succeeds, the DSP is known to
be done with everything for this process, so the worker reference is
dropped too. If it doesn't succeed, the context is left exactly where
a real response or a future SSR would still find it: still linked on
fl->interrupted, and fl is left on cctx->users (the unlink moves from
fastrpc_device_release() into fastrpc_user_free(), which only runs
once fl truly has no references left) so fastrpc_notify_users() can
still walk down to it later.

The same unreachable-orphan problem exists for a timed-out kernel
invoke (-ETIMEDOUT, only possible when kernel=true): the bail: path
used to unconditionally unlink the context from fl->pending before
dropping its reference, even though the DSP may still answer later.
Leave it linked on fl->pending in that case instead, so
fastrpc_notify_users() can still find and release it if the channel
is torn down before that answer arrives.

Remove the obsolete invoke_interrupted_mmaps mechanism from
fastrpc_channel_ctx; context resources are now kept alive through the
context refcount rather than by migrating mmaps to a channel-level
list.

This also includes "misc: fastrpc: drop the DSP-response reference on
channel removal" from the same series:

fastrpc_invoke_send() takes a second reference on the invoke context
before calling rpmsg_send(), to be dropped later by whichever side
consumes the eventual DSP response: fastrpc_rpmsg_callback() for a
real reply, via schedule_work(&ctx->put_work).

fastrpc_notify_users(), called from fastrpc_rpmsg_remove() when the
rpmsg channel goes away (e.g. on subsystem restart), only completes
each pending context with -EPIPE so its waiter can return - it never
schedules the matching put. Since the channel is gone, no response
will ever arrive to trigger it through the normal callback path
either. Every context that was genuinely in flight (successfully
sent, not yet answered) at the time of an SSR therefore leaks that
second reference, and with it the context, its DMA maps, and the
reference it holds on fl and cctx.

Schedule the same put_work that a real response would have
triggered, exactly as fastrpc_rpmsg_callback() does. rpmsg guarantees
.callback and .remove are mutually exclusive for a given device, so
there is no concurrent real response that could race with this.

Fixes: c68cfb718c8f ("misc: fastrpc: Add support for context Invoke method")
Fixes: 387f625585d1 ("misc: fastrpc: handle interrupted contexts")
Co-developed-by: Srinivas Kandagatla <srinivas.kandagatla@oss.qualcomm.com>
Signed-off-by: Srinivas Kandagatla <srinivas.kandagatla@oss.qualcomm.com>
Signed-off-by: Anandu Krishnan E <anandu.e@oss.qualcomm.com>
[Akisaira: this combines patches 2/3 and 3/3 of the v4 series, as 2/3 alone
 puts contexts twice and can leave freed ones linked on fl->pending.
 Changes from v4, which the text above doesn't reflect:
 - put_work_scheduled also stays set before fastrpc_invoke_send() takes the
   worker reference, and a failed send claims it before dropping it.
   Otherwise fastrpc_notify_users() drops a reference that doesn't exist:
   on SSR glink wakes a sender blocked on the crashed DSP before it removes
   the channel, so the failed send and notify_users() both put the context.
 - A context is parked only when the wait itself was interrupted. Checking
   err at the bail: label also caught a DSP retval of -ERESTARTSYS and
   parked a call that had already completed. Setup and send failures go
   to bail: again.
 - At close, the worker reference of a parked context stays until the DSP
   answers or the channel goes away, even if the process release
   succeeded: that doesn't prove the DSP is done with the call's buffers.
 - fastrpc_context_schedule_put_work() uses xchg() instead of fl->lock.
   fastrpc_rpmsg_callback() runs in hard interrupt context with glink over
   smem and fl->lock isn't IRQ-safe, so taking it there can deadlock on one
   CPU. fastrpc_notify_users() also calls it with fl->lock already held,
   which would deadlock on channel removal whenever a context is pending or
   parked.
 - A retry resumes only with the same args, as ctx->args isn't repointed on
   resume. A different retry is a new call.
 - invoke_interrupted_mmaps stays, now filled at close if a context is still
   parked. A static PD outlives its client and may still use fl->mmaps, and
   dropping the parked context at close, or its late reply, frees fl. That
   is what 76e8e4ace1ed guards against.
 - fastrpc_rpmsg_remove() walks invoke_interrupted_mmaps under cctx->lock.
   Close now adds to that list, and misc_deregister() doesn't wait for open
   fds.
 - rpmsg keeps .callback and .remove apart on SSR only because glink_smem
   disables its IRQ first; it doesn't on unbind or a remote close.
   put_work_scheduled keeps the put single either way.]
Signed-off-by: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: backport, https://patch.msgid.link/20261117-interrupted-invoke-v4-v4-2-2320a8a7eda2@oss.qualcomm.com, https://patch.msgid.link/20261117-interrupted-invoke-v4-v4-3-2320a8a7eda2@oss.qualcomm.com
Upstream-Status: Backport [https://patch.msgid.link/20261117-interrupted-invoke-v4-v4-2-2320a8a7eda2@oss.qualcomm.com, https://patch.msgid.link/20261117-interrupted-invoke-v4-v4-3-2320a8a7eda2@oss.qualcomm.com]
---
 drivers/misc/fastrpc.c | 203 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 175 insertions(+), 28 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
index 7e84230d2e..53aa7254c8 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
@@ -261,6 +261,7 @@ struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx {
 	int pid;
 	int client_id;
 	u32 sc;
+	u32 handle;
 	u64 *fdlist;
 	u32 *crc;
 	/* Poll memory that DSP updates */
@@ -271,6 +272,12 @@ struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx {
 	bool is_work_done;
 	/* process updates poll memory instead of glink response */
 	bool is_polled;
+	/*
+	 * Set while nobody may drop the worker reference: before
+	 * fastrpc_invoke_send() takes it, and once a reply, notify_users() or a
+	 * failed send has claimed it.
+	 */
+	bool put_work_scheduled;
 	struct kref refcount;
 	struct list_head node; /* list of ctxs */
 	struct completion work;
@@ -340,6 +347,7 @@ struct fastrpc_user {
 	struct list_head user;
 	struct list_head maps;
 	struct list_head pending;
+	struct list_head interrupted;
 	struct list_head mmaps;
 
 	struct fastrpc_channel_ctx *cctx;
@@ -559,8 +567,15 @@ static void fastrpc_context_put(struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *ctx);
 static void fastrpc_user_free(struct kref *ref)
 {
 	struct fastrpc_user *fl = container_of(ref, struct fastrpc_user, refcount);
+	struct fastrpc_channel_ctx *cctx = fl->cctx;
 	struct fastrpc_map *map, *m;
 	struct fastrpc_buf *buf, *b;
+	unsigned long flags;
+
+	/* fl stays on cctx->users until this point, so an SSR before fd close can still find it. */
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&cctx->lock, flags);
+	list_del(&fl->user);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&cctx->lock, flags);
 
 	fastrpc_buf_free(fl->init_mem);
 
@@ -572,7 +587,7 @@ static void fastrpc_user_free(struct kref *ref)
 		fastrpc_buf_free(buf);
 	}
 
-	fastrpc_channel_ctx_put(fl->cctx);
+	fastrpc_channel_ctx_put(cctx);
 	mutex_destroy(&fl->mutex);
 	kfree(fl);
 }
@@ -599,6 +614,12 @@ static void fastrpc_context_free(struct kref *ref)
 	cctx = ctx->cctx;
 	fl = ctx->fl;
 
+	/* list_del_init(), not list_del(): a concurrent drain may still check list_empty(). */
+	spin_lock(&fl->lock);
+	if (!list_empty(&ctx->node))
+		list_del_init(&ctx->node);
+	spin_unlock(&fl->lock);
+
 	for (i = 0; i < ctx->nbufs; i++)
 		fastrpc_map_put(ctx->maps[i]);
 
@@ -610,6 +631,7 @@ static void fastrpc_context_free(struct kref *ref)
 
 	kfree(ctx->maps);
 	kfree(ctx->olaps);
+	kfree(ctx->args);
 	kfree(ctx);
 
 	/* Release the reference taken in fastrpc_context_alloc() */
@@ -635,6 +657,59 @@ static void fastrpc_context_put_wq(struct work_struct *work)
 	fastrpc_context_put(ctx);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Schedules put_work at most once per ctx; racing callers may both try.
+ * No fl->lock here: fastrpc_rpmsg_callback() runs in hard interrupt context
+ * with glink over smem, where the non-IRQ-safe fl->lock can deadlock, and
+ * fastrpc_notify_users() calls this with fl->lock already held.
+ */
+static void fastrpc_context_schedule_put_work(struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *ctx)
+{
+	if (!xchg(&ctx->put_work_scheduled, true))
+		schedule_work(&ctx->put_work);
+}
+
+/* References held by ctx are left intact; only list membership changes. */
+static void fastrpc_context_save_interrupted(struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *ctx)
+{
+	spin_lock(&ctx->fl->lock);
+	list_move_tail(&ctx->node, &ctx->fl->interrupted);
+	spin_unlock(&ctx->fl->lock);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Matches on handle, sc and args: the restarted syscall passes all of them
+ * unchanged. Anything else is a different call, whose buffers the DSP's output
+ * wouldn't reach, as ctx->args still holds the interrupted call's.
+ */
+static struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *
+fastrpc_context_restore_interrupted(struct fastrpc_user *fl, u32 handle,
+				    u32 sc, struct fastrpc_invoke_args *args)
+{
+	struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *ctx = NULL, *ictx;
+
+	spin_lock(&fl->lock);
+	list_for_each_entry(ictx, &fl->interrupted, node) {
+		if (ictx->pid == current->pid && ictx->handle == handle &&
+		    ictx->sc == sc &&
+		    (!ictx->nscalars ||
+		     !memcmp(ictx->args, args, ictx->nscalars * sizeof(*args)))) {
+			/* May already be mid-teardown; don't resurrect it. */
+			if (kref_get_unless_zero(&ictx->refcount)) {
+				ctx = ictx;
+				list_move_tail(&ctx->node, &fl->pending);
+			}
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+	spin_unlock(&fl->lock);
+
+	if (ctx)
+		fastrpc_context_put(ctx);
+
+	return ctx;
+}
+
 #define CMP(aa, bb) ((aa) == (bb) ? 0 : (aa) < (bb) ? -1 : 1)
 static int olaps_cmp(const void *a, const void *b)
 {
@@ -685,7 +760,7 @@ static void fastrpc_get_buff_overlaps(struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *ctx)
 }
 
 static struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *fastrpc_context_alloc(
-			struct fastrpc_user *user, u32 kernel, u32 sc,
+			struct fastrpc_user *user, u32 kernel, u32 handle, u32 sc,
 			struct fastrpc_invoke_args *args)
 {
 	struct fastrpc_channel_ctx *cctx = user->cctx;
@@ -715,7 +790,15 @@ static struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *fastrpc_context_alloc(
 			kfree(ctx);
 			return ERR_PTR(-ENOMEM);
 		}
-		ctx->args = args;
+		/* Own copy: ctx may outlive the caller's args if parked on fl->interrupted. */
+		ctx->args = kzalloc_objs(*ctx->args, ctx->nscalars);
+		if (!ctx->args) {
+			kfree(ctx->olaps);
+			kfree(ctx->maps);
+			kfree(ctx);
+			return ERR_PTR(-ENOMEM);
+		}
+		memcpy(ctx->args, args, ctx->nscalars * sizeof(*args));
 		fastrpc_get_buff_overlaps(ctx);
 	}
 
@@ -725,12 +808,15 @@ static struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *fastrpc_context_alloc(
 	fastrpc_user_get(user);
 
 	ctx->sc = sc;
+	ctx->handle = handle;
 	ctx->retval = -1;
 	ctx->pid = current->pid;
 	ctx->client_id = user->client_id;
 	ctx->cctx = cctx;
 	init_completion(&ctx->work);
 	INIT_WORK(&ctx->put_work, fastrpc_context_put_wq);
+	/* No worker reference yet: notify_users() must not drop one. */
+	ctx->put_work_scheduled = true;
 
 	spin_lock(&user->lock);
 	list_add_tail(&ctx->node, &user->pending);
@@ -759,6 +845,7 @@ static struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *fastrpc_context_alloc(
 	fastrpc_channel_ctx_put(cctx);
 	kfree(ctx->maps);
 	kfree(ctx->olaps);
+	kfree(ctx->args);
 	kfree(ctx);
 
 	return ERR_PTR(ret);
@@ -1269,10 +1356,16 @@ static int fastrpc_invoke_send(struct fastrpc_session_ctx *sctx,
 	msg->addr = ctx->buf ? ctx->buf->dma_addr : 0;
 	msg->size = roundup(ctx->msg_sz, PAGE_SIZE);
 	fastrpc_context_get(ctx);
+	/* Ordered after the get, so whoever claims the reference sees it. */
+	smp_store_release(&ctx->put_work_scheduled, false);
 
 	ret = rpmsg_send(cctx->rpdev->ept, (void *)msg, sizeof(*msg));
 
-	if (ret)
+	/*
+	 * Nothing was sent, so no reply will drop the worker reference. Drop it
+	 * here unless fastrpc_notify_users() has already claimed it.
+	 */
+	if (ret && !xchg(&ctx->put_work_scheduled, true))
 		fastrpc_context_put(ctx);
 
 	return ret;
@@ -1349,8 +1442,6 @@ static int fastrpc_internal_invoke(struct fastrpc_user *fl,  u32 kernel,
 				   struct fastrpc_invoke_args *args)
 {
 	struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *ctx = NULL;
-	struct fastrpc_buf *buf, *b;
-
 	int err = 0;
 
 	if (!fl->sctx)
@@ -1364,7 +1455,13 @@ static int fastrpc_internal_invoke(struct fastrpc_user *fl,  u32 kernel,
 		return -EPERM;
 	}
 
-	ctx = fastrpc_context_alloc(fl, kernel, sc, args);
+	/* Resume an interrupted invoke instead of resending to the DSP. */
+	if (!kernel)
+		ctx = fastrpc_context_restore_interrupted(fl, handle, sc, args);
+	if (ctx)
+		goto wait;
+
+	ctx = fastrpc_context_alloc(fl, kernel, handle, sc, args);
 	if (IS_ERR(ctx))
 		return PTR_ERR(ctx);
 
@@ -1386,7 +1483,24 @@ static int fastrpc_internal_invoke(struct fastrpc_user *fl,  u32 kernel,
 	if (handle > FASTRPC_MAX_STATIC_HANDLE && fl->pd == USER_PD && fl->poll_mode)
 		ctx->is_polled = true;
 
+wait:
 	err = fastrpc_wait_for_completion(ctx, kernel);
+	if (err == -ERESTARTSYS) {
+		dev_dbg(fl->sctx->dev,
+			"fastrpc_invoke: INTERRUPTED ctxid=0x%llx handle=0x%x nscalars=%d\n",
+			ctx->ctxid, handle, ctx->nscalars);
+		/* Still with the DSP: park it so that a retry resumes the wait. */
+		fastrpc_context_save_interrupted(ctx);
+		return err;
+	}
+	if (err == -ETIMEDOUT) {
+		dev_warn(fl->sctx->dev,
+			 "fastrpc_invoke: TIMEOUT ctxid=0x%llx handle=0x%x nscalars=%d\n",
+			 ctx->ctxid, handle, ctx->nscalars);
+		/* Leave linked: a late DSP answer or notify_users() still needs to reach it. */
+		fastrpc_context_put(ctx);
+		return err;
+	}
 	if (err)
 		goto bail;
 
@@ -1403,22 +1517,11 @@ static int fastrpc_internal_invoke(struct fastrpc_user *fl,  u32 kernel,
 		goto bail;
 
 bail:
-	if (err != -ERESTARTSYS && err != -ETIMEDOUT) {
-		/* We are done with this compute context */
-		spin_lock(&fl->lock);
-		list_del(&ctx->node);
-		spin_unlock(&fl->lock);
-		fastrpc_context_put(ctx);
-	}
-
-	if (err == -ERESTARTSYS) {
-		spin_lock(&fl->lock);
-		list_for_each_entry_safe(buf, b, &fl->mmaps, node) {
-			list_del(&buf->node);
-			list_add_tail(&buf->node, &fl->cctx->invoke_interrupted_mmaps);
-		}
-		spin_unlock(&fl->lock);
-	}
+	/* The call is over: it completed, failed before sending, or the DSP returned an error. */
+	spin_lock(&fl->lock);
+	list_del_init(&ctx->node);
+	spin_unlock(&fl->lock);
+	fastrpc_context_put(ctx);
 
 	if (err)
 		dev_dbg(fl->sctx->dev, "Error: Invoke Failed %d\n", err);
@@ -1719,14 +1822,44 @@ static int fastrpc_device_release(struct inode *inode, struct file *file)
 {
 	struct fastrpc_user *fl = (struct fastrpc_user *)file->private_data;
 	struct fastrpc_channel_ctx *cctx = fl->cctx;
+	struct fastrpc_invoke_ctx *ctx, *n;
 	unsigned long flags;
+	LIST_HEAD(mmaps);
 
 	fastrpc_release_current_dsp_process(fl);
 
+	/*
+	 * A call still parked means the client died mid-invoke, and a static PD
+	 * outlives it and may still use fl->mmaps. Dropping the parked call
+	 * below, or its late reply, can free fl, so keep them with the channel.
+	 * cctx->lock isn't taken under fl->lock, as fastrpc_notify_users()
+	 * nests them the other way.
+	 */
+	spin_lock(&fl->lock);
+	if (!list_empty(&fl->interrupted))
+		list_splice_init(&fl->mmaps, &mmaps);
+	spin_unlock(&fl->lock);
+
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&cctx->lock, flags);
-	list_del(&fl->user);
+	list_splice_tail(&mmaps, &cctx->invoke_interrupted_mmaps);
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&cctx->lock, flags);
 
+	/*
+	 * Drop only the invoker ref: no retry can arrive once the fd is closed.
+	 * The worker ref stays, and ctx stays linked, until the DSP answers or
+	 * the channel goes away. A successful process release doesn't prove
+	 * that the DSP is done with this call's buffers.
+	 */
+	spin_lock(&fl->lock);
+	list_for_each_entry_safe(ctx, n, &fl->interrupted, node) {
+		ctx->retval = -EPIPE;
+		complete(&ctx->work);
+		spin_unlock(&fl->lock);
+		fastrpc_context_put(ctx);
+		spin_lock(&fl->lock);
+	}
+	spin_unlock(&fl->lock);
+
 	fastrpc_session_free(cctx, fl->sctx);
 	file->private_data = NULL;
 	/* Release the reference taken in fastrpc_device_open */
@@ -1756,6 +1889,7 @@ static int fastrpc_device_open(struct inode *inode, struct file *filp)
 	spin_lock_init(&fl->lock);
 	mutex_init(&fl->mutex);
 	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&fl->pending);
+	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&fl->interrupted);
 	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&fl->maps);
 	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&fl->mmaps);
 	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&fl->user);
@@ -2658,6 +2792,16 @@ static void fastrpc_notify_users(struct fastrpc_user *user)
 	list_for_each_entry(ctx, &user->pending, node) {
 		ctx->retval = -EPIPE;
 		complete(&ctx->work);
+		fastrpc_context_schedule_put_work(ctx);
+	}
+	/*
+	 * Drops only the worker ref. The invoker ref of a parked context goes
+	 * when the call resumes or in fastrpc_device_release().
+	 */
+	list_for_each_entry(ctx, &user->interrupted, node) {
+		ctx->retval = -EPIPE;
+		complete(&ctx->work);
+		fastrpc_context_schedule_put_work(ctx);
 	}
 	spin_unlock(&user->lock);
 }
@@ -2682,8 +2826,11 @@ static void fastrpc_rpmsg_remove(struct rpmsg_device *rpdev)
 	if (cctx->secure_fdevice)
 		misc_deregister(&cctx->secure_fdevice->miscdev);
 
+	/* misc_deregister() doesn't wait for open fds, and a close adds to this list. */
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&cctx->lock, flags);
 	list_for_each_entry_safe(buf, b, &cctx->invoke_interrupted_mmaps, node)
 		list_del(&buf->node);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&cctx->lock, flags);
 
 	if (cctx->remote_heap_size && cctx->vmcount) {
 		u64 src_perms = 0;
@@ -2766,11 +2913,11 @@ static int fastrpc_rpmsg_callback(struct rpmsg_device *rpdev, void *data,
 	complete(&ctx->work);
 
 	/*
-	 * The DMA buffer associated with the context cannot be freed in
-	 * interrupt context so schedule it through a worker thread to
-	 * avoid a kernel BUG.
+	 * Can't free the DMA buffer in interrupt context; defer to a
+	 * worker. Guarded: notify_users()/device_release() may also be
+	 * racing to schedule this for the same ctx.
 	 */
-	schedule_work(&ctx->put_work);
+	fastrpc_context_schedule_put_work(ctx);
 
 	return 0;
 }
